+# Decision: Concurrency Reconciliation for Independently Edited Visits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited by more than one field worker before either edit reaches the server: tablets are offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. When two edits to the same visit both arrive, the record store has to decide what becomes the visit's usable current state without discarding either submission and without inventing authority from arrival order.
+
+A visit's fields are not independent in ways the server can detect from their names alone. Some edits are unrelated — correcting an observer name, adding a missed species. Others are scientifically coupled — revising a species identification and its cover estimate are two edits to two different fields that only make sense together. The record store has no botanical knowledge and cannot tell these cases apart by inspecting which fields changed.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates (inherited):**
+
+- Must not discard a submitted edit — the project's `Do not silently discard a submitted edit` tenet applies to every revision, resolved or not.
+- Must remain operable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator — the project's tenet on operability.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Scientific-relationship safety (major)** — turns on the tenet `Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable`: can the mechanism combine two submissions in a way that crosses a scientific relationship between fields without anyone having reviewed it?
+- **Review workload (moderate)** — how much of the 6,000 visits/season volume lands in the coordinators' review queue versus flowing straight through.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden (moderate)** — how much logic the record store must own and keep correct as the visit schema evolves (new fields, new species-entry shapes).
+- **Submission envelope simplicity (minor)** — how much structure a tablet has to construct and a reviewer has to read per edit.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision it started from (its base revision ID). A submission whose base matches the visit's current revision becomes the new current revision. A submission whose base has since been superseded is stored but left unresolved, alongside every other revision unresolved against the same base, until a coordinator picks one to become current. The record store never inspects or recombines individual fields — a revision is accepted or held for review as a whole.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Discard-nothing gate: **passes** — every revision, chosen or not, is retained permanently.
+- Operability gate: **passes** — coordinators work a review queue of whole revisions; no rule catalog to administer.
+- Scientific-relationship safety (major): **strong fit** — the mechanism never combines fields, so it cannot cross a relationship it doesn't understand. A coordinator who resolves a visit sees each full submission, including the species ID and cover estimate together if one worker changed both.
+- Review workload (moderate): **weak fit** — two workers editing unrelated fields on the same visit still produce unresolved work, even though nothing about their edits actually conflicts.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden (moderate): **strong fit** — the server owns a revision pointer and a base comparison; nothing to maintain as the visit schema grows.
+- Submission envelope simplicity (minor): **strong fit** — a tablet adds one field, the base revision ID, to what it already submits.
+
+**Summary:** Keeps every submission whole and inspectable at the cost of review work for edits that don't actually conflict.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies its base snapshot and a structured set of field-level changes. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new revision; changes that touch the same field go to review. Combining disjoint changes requires maintained merge rules per field shape — scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries each need their own rule.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Discard-nothing gate: **passes** — originals are retained alongside the merged result.
+- Operability gate: **passes** on its face — coordinators still work a review queue — but the merge rules themselves need a technically fluent maintainer as the schema changes, a standing cost the whole-visit option doesn't carry.
+- Scientific-relationship safety (major): **weak fit** — this is the option's central exposure. A revised species identification and its revised cover estimate are changes to two different fields; a disjoint-field merge rule combines them automatically because they don't collide, even though they are scientifically coupled. The server has no way to encode that coupling from field names.
+- Review workload (moderate): **strong fit** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach the queue, so most concurrent edits flow straight to export-eligible state.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden (moderate): **weak fit** — requires and maintains merge rules for scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries, and those rules need revisiting whenever the visit schema changes.
+- Submission envelope simplicity (minor): **partial fit** — a tablet must construct a structured change set against a base snapshot rather than a single base pointer.
+
+**Summary:** Shrinks the review queue and speeds more visits to export, but the auto-merge path is exactly the mechanism that can silently cross a scientific relationship the system was never told about.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the major criterion, scientific-relationship safety, which in turn is where the project's inspectability tenet actually bites: an exported value has to be traceable to a submission a human stood behind, not to a merge rule's guess that two field edits were independent. Field-aware merging fails this exactly where it matters most — unrelated-looking field edits (a species ID, its cover estimate) are precisely the case the record store cannot evaluate safely, and getting it wrong there is invisible, not just costly. The team accepted the moderate, visible cost — a larger review queue, including review work for edits that don't actually conflict — over a mechanism that trades that cost for an occasional silent, undetectable error.
+
+This forecloses automatic combination of concurrent edits in the first release: every visit with more than one unresolved revision goes to a coordinator, regardless of which fields changed. It does not foreclose field-aware merging permanently — if review volume proves unsustainable at the project's scale (24 workers, ~6,000 visits/season) and the team identifies specific field relationships it's confident are safe to automate, that could reopen this decision with rules scoped to a vetted set of fields. Today no such vetted set exists, and the record store has no way to derive one from field names alone, so that option stays rejected rather than deferred-and-planned.
+
+It obligates both downstream nodes to build around the revision model rather than their own concurrency logic:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID for each local draft and attach it to the next submission for that visit, and must be able to tell a worker that their submission became unresolved review work rather than simply confirming success or silently losing to another edit.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision-comparison view over a visit's unresolved revisions, a resolution action that records the coordinator's identity and the IDs of every contributing revision, and an export-eligibility rule that holds any visit with unresolved work out of the Friday CSV.
