+## Revision Model
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Each accepted submission creates one **revision** of its visit: the full payload, the author, the server receipt time, a server-generated revision ID, and the **base revision ID** the worker edited from (absent for a visit's first submission).
+
+Each visit tracks one **current revision** — the revision considered the visit's usable state. A submission is checked against the visit's current revision at acceptance time:
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+- If the submission's base revision ID equals the current revision ID, the new revision becomes the current revision. No review work is created.
+- If it does not — another revision became current after this worker fetched their copy — the new revision is accepted and stored, but the current revision does not change. The visit now has **unresolved review work**: the current revision and every revision submitted against a base that was superseded before it arrived.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+A visit can accumulate more than one unresolved revision if multiple workers edit concurrently against the same superseded base. All of them stay unresolved, and all of them are retained, until a coordinator resolves the visit.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+**Resolution** is a coordinator action on a visit with unresolved work: the coordinator picks one of the unresolved revisions (which may or may not be the existing current revision) to become the current revision. The revisions not chosen are retained as history but no longer count as unresolved. The record store stores the coordinator's identity and the IDs of every revision that was part of the unresolved set with the resolution.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+No revision is ever discarded or rewritten by this process — resolution only moves the current-revision pointer and clears unresolved status.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Submission and Retrieval
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve every submitted revision's original payload; the record store never writes a combined or edited representation over it. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Retrieving a visit returns its current revision and whether unresolved review work exists. A caller that needs the unresolved revisions themselves (for coordinator review) fetches submission history, which returns every revision in acceptance order with its base revision ID, author, and receipt time.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+This node defines the submission envelope (payload, idempotency key, base revision ID), the current-state response (current revision plus an unresolved-work indicator), the submission-history response (every revision with its base), and the resolution operation (coordinator ID, chosen revision ID, contributing revision IDs).
+
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope: it retains the base revision ID for a visit's next edit and will need a way to show a worker that their submission is now part of unresolved work rather than silently superseded. Review-export sizes its coordinator interface around the unresolved-work representation: a revision-comparison view over the unresolved set, a resolution action, and export eligibility rules that hold a visit with unresolved work out of the CSV. Both link back to this node rather than redefine revision semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency reconciliation for independently edited visits | Whole-visit revisions: an edit names its base revision; a mismatched base creates unresolved review work; a coordinator picks the current revision | Field-aware three-way merge: disjoint field edits combine automatically, overlapping edits go to review | See `decisions/concurrency-reconciliation.md`. |
